menu "Thermopost"

config THERMOPOST_RUN_BRINGUP_PROBE
    bool "Run the hardware bring-up probe at boot"
    default n
    help
        Runs main/bringup_probe.cpp before the normal boot sequence: identifies
        the SoC, scans I2C, reads the LCD controller ID, blinks the backlight,
        and self-tests storage and settings, ending in a GATE PASS / GATE FAIL
        summary. Turn this on when re-checking the pin map against hardware or
        after rewiring.

        DESTRUCTIVE: it erases the temperature log and resets settings to
        defaults so its checks start from a known state. It also parks the boot
        forever if any check fails, so the app will not come up.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_OFFSET_X
    int "Touch X offset, in pixels"
    default 0
    range -40 40
    help
        Added to every reported touch X, in screen coordinates, after the
        controller's swap/mirror transform. Positive moves the reported point
        right.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_OFFSET_Y
    int "Touch Y offset, in pixels"
    default -6
    range -40 40
    help
        Added to every reported touch Y, in screen coordinates, after the
        controller's swap/mirror transform. Positive moves the reported point
        DOWN the screen; negative moves it UP.

        Defaults to -6 because taps register slightly below where they are aimed.
        Two things cause that and both push the same way: the touch glass is
        bonded over the LCD with a small vertical offset, and a fingertip's
        contact patch sits below the point the user believes they are pointing
        at.

        Dial it in with THERMOPOST_TOUCH_LOG rather than by feel. If the error
        is roughly the same at the top and the bottom of the screen it is an
        offset and this fixes it; if it grows towards one edge the problem is a
        scale mismatch in the touch config's x_max/y_max instead, and this would
        only hide it in the middle.

config THERMOPOST_TOUCH_LOG
    bool "Log every touch point"
    default n
    help
        Logs the raw controller reading and the adjusted screen coordinate on
        every press, so a known target can be tapped and the difference read off.
        Presses are human-paced, so this costs nothing while it is on.

# --- Audio ------------------------------------------------------------------

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_TEST_SECONDS
    int "Length of the Sound page's test tone, in seconds"
    default 2
    range 1 60
    help
        Two seconds is the right length for judging a working speaker and the
        wrong length for probing a silent one — there are six pins to meter and
        the tone is over before the second probe lands. Raise it while chasing a
        fault, put it back afterwards.

config THERMOPOST_AUDIO_DEBUG
    bool "Dump codec registers and log every tone"
    default n
    help
        Dumps the ES8311's register file after the DAC is powered up, and logs
        each tone's volume, requested decibels and sample count. The register
        dump is the difference between "the codec is not playing" and "the codec
        was never configured", which look the same from outside — a distinction
        that mattered enough to find this board's three wrong pin facts, and will
        matter again on the next board revision.

# --- Alarm ------------------------------------------------------------------

config THERMOPOST_ALERT_SNOOZE_MINUTES
    int "How long the alarm's Snooze button silences it, in minutes"
    default 5
    range 1 240
    help
        Snooze quiets the alarm — panel and tone — and lets it come back if the
        temperature is still outside the band when the time is up. The other
        button, Off, is the one that stays quiet until the reading changes what
        it is doing.

        Five minutes is a walk to the tank and back. The right number depends on
        how far away the tank is: long enough to be doing something about it,
        short enough that walking away instead is not the same as switching the
        alarm off.
# --- Power ------------------------------------------------------------------

config THERMOPOST_POWER_DIM_SECONDS
    int "Idle seconds before the backlight dims"
    default 60
    range 5 3600
    help
        The first rung of the idle ladder. Nothing but the backlight changes —
        the sensor keeps sampling, the recorder keeps writing and the alarm keeps
        watching, and an alarm takes the screen straight back to full.

config THERMOPOST_POWER_DARK_SECONDS
    int "Idle seconds before the panel blanks"
    default 180
    range 10 7200
    help
        The second rung, and it must be larger than the dim delay or the dim tier
        is unreachable (power.cpp static_asserts it). The panel stops scanning and
        the framebuffer survives, so a touch brings back the screen that was
        already there rather than a repaint from nothing.

config THERMOPOST_POWER_DIM_PERCENT
    int "Backlight level in the dim tier, percent"
    default 20
    range 1 99
    help
        Low enough to be worth doing and high enough to still read the number
        from arm's length. Worth checking on hardware before trusting: GPIO45 is
        documented by the wiki as a plain high/low enable, so if the circuit gates
        a fixed-current driver the intermediate duty cycles may not dim at all and
        this tier collapses into on/off.

endmenu
